  "worked_solution": "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+10y-24 & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}-10y+24) & =0 \\\\ -(y-4)(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =4,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=4$ and $y=6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,4)$ | $(4,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y-4)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (4,6),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b123be00d466cf117b6c3f19376230e240a6ef0a64677b5d18457d43d736fd94.png)",
